{"authored" [{"question" "q-37609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-3I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4286a7677ab3c62ffb9e374117e194de0d203869cbdc668cbdd42a9e42c489bf" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-37084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 3 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-(3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9864dfbd9c513e1a393896ab51147b48acf9da8a4c20fe9c0f49d214fc6ee183"} {"question" "q-37084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9864dfbd9c513e1a393896ab51147b48acf9da8a4c20fe9c0f49d214fc6ee183"} {"question" "q-37086" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37086" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37086" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-3I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9864dfbd9c513e1a393896ab51147b48acf9da8a4c20fe9c0f49d214fc6ee183"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-37609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-140038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-140038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-140038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9864dfbd9c513e1a393896ab51147b48acf9da8a4c20fe9c0f49d214fc6ee183"} {"question" "q-140038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-140038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-140038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-140054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-140054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-(-6)\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-49+48 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071856/state.json" "file_sha256" "84cab5805181a10352eddbb603e2e005ef88eef79fc6710a029fb7815299c34a"} {"question" "q-140054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cours